    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>say</strong> is followed by the words or by <em>that</em>: <em>She said hello. He said that he was tired.</em> <strong>tell</strong> needs a person: <em>She told me a story. He told us to wait.</em> You cannot say <em>say me</em> or <em>tell hello</em>. Fixed phrases: <em>tell the truth, tell a joke, tell the time</em> and <em>say sorry, say goodbye</em>.",
        "tag": "say or tell"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>borrow</strong> means you take something and give it back later: <em>Can I borrow your pen?</em> <strong>lend</strong> means you give something for a short time: <em>Can you lend me your pen? Tom lent Amira his bike.</em> The person with the thing <em>lends</em>. The person who needs it <em>borrows</em>.",
        "tag": "borrow or lend"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>rise</strong> means to go up by itself. It has no object: <em>The sun rises at six. Prices rose last year.</em> <strong>raise</strong> means to lift something or make it higher. It needs an object: <em>She raised her hand. The shop raised its prices.</em>",
        "tag": "rise or raise"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>lie</strong> means to be flat or in a lying position. It has no object: <em>I lie on the sofa. The keys are lying on the table.</em> <strong>lay</strong> means to put something down. It needs an object: <em>Lay the book on the table. She laid the baby in the bed.</em> The forms are tricky: <em>lie – lay – lain</em> and <em>lay – laid – laid</em>. Note: <em>lay</em> is also the past of <em>lie</em>: <em>He lay on the sofa.</em>",
        "tag": "lie or lay"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>She said me the news.</em> → ✓ <em>She told me the news.</em> · ✗ <em>Can you borrow me your pen?</em> → ✓ <em>Can you lend me your pen?</em> · ✗ <em>I'm going to lay on the sofa.</em> → ✓ <em>I'm going to lie on the sofa.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "There is a second verb <em>lie</em>: it means to say something that is not true. It is regular: <em>lie – lied – lied</em> (<em>He lied to me.</em>). It is a different verb, so don't mix it up with <em>lie – lay – lain</em>. <em>Lie</em> and <em>lay</em> are hard for native speakers too, so don't worry if you need time. In this topic we use mostly the present tense. Past forms are in the overview. For how past forms work, see <a href=\"/perfekt\">Past simple</a> and <a href=\"/zusammengesetzte-verben\">Present perfect or past simple</a>. For <em>say</em> and <em>tell</em> in reported speech, see <a href=\"/funktionen-von-werden\">Reported speech</a>. US: a pay <em>rise</em> in British English is a pay <em>raise</em> in American English.",
    "reference": {
